A||||20||Monkeyman||Pulls to the left---HELP!!!!||||1||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||08-22-2000||08:20 PM||||Brake upgrade is done, new MC, brakes bled, etc.<P>Test drove it.  I still have a mushy pedal and it pulls to the left (only when braking) very badly!!  Does this just mean that I need to re-bleed the brakes???  What else could it be??  I can drive the car the way it is around town, but I sure won't go out of town in this condition.  The brakes aren't even as good as the old ones.  I need to leave town on business either Thursday am or Friday am so I have to get this fixed by then.  I bled the brakes farthest (right rear) to closest (left front).  I bled then until no bubbles came through the hose (using a piece of clear hose over the bleeder valve so I don't get any excess fluid on the ground.  I did get a bunch of brake fluid on the right front (forgot to close the valve).  I drove about 4 blocks with my foot on the brakes hoping to clean it off.<P>Sorry for the bandwidth, but please help me.||||reg||1
Z||000001||JohnnyK||08-22-2000||08:28 PM||||Doesn't sound like a bleeding problem (well, the mushy pedal does), but the pulling would be on account of the left side brakes grabbing harder than the right. Why, I don't know, but I'm sure someone does.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||08-22-2000||08:37 PM||||I should have stated what I have on the Grape Ape now.  Rotors, calipers, etc from an '88 Grand Am (fronts are the Fiero Store kit) and a MC from a full size Blazer.<P>I know that the problem is that the left front is grabbing more than the right, but I don't know why.  I need to get this straightened out fast.  I'll re-bleed the brakes tomorrow after work (very time consuming since I have to pull one wheel off, bleed that brake, put the wheel back on, move to the next one).  Hopefully this will straighten out the mushy pedal, but I still don't know what to do with the pulling.||||reg||1
Z||000003||webbee||08-22-2000||09:14 PM||||Did you bench bleed the master cylinder befor hooking it up to the lines? If not you need to do that first. You can crack the lines, wrap them with a paper towel, put a tray under the cylinder to catch the out flow and bleed it in the car. Tighten the lines and rebleed the cylinders.<P>You can buy a vacume bleeder and do it that way, too. Less messy, more costly.<P>This will get rid of the mushy feel and may eliminate the pulling.<P>Congrats on doing the job, you're almost there!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||08-22-2000||09:56 PM||||I did bench bleed the MC.  The instructions told me to install a couple of plastic plugs, then fill the MC and keep depressing the plunger until I saw no more bubbles.  I did that.  However, I did notice at one point as my wife was pumping the pedal that the front part of the reservoir was very low.  I filled it up and carried on.  I have no problem re bleeding the MC and rebleeding the brakes.  Although time consuming, if that's all it takes to get great brakes, it's worth it.  I'll just chock it up to experience.  What's worrying me is the fact that it pulls so bad.  Doesn't everything equalize in the system?  As far as I can tell, all of the parts are in the correct place (I forgot to add the anti-squeek stuff on the left front--no biggie).  Like I said, it travels straight until I hit the brakes.  Makes me think that it's a braking problem instead of a bearing problem, right????||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||08-22-2000||10:02 PM||||Sounds like you need new rubber brake hoses on the front.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||08-22-2000||10:08 PM||||Cooter--I definitely need new hoses, but I didn't have this problem with my old brakes.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||08-22-2000||10:24 PM||||Could be one of the new calipers is DOA or crud from the lines got into it and now it binds.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||08-22-2000||10:28 PM||||Could be, but while my wife was pumping the brakes (after I closed the bleeder valve) I checked to make sure they were doing their thing.  They were.  I'd hate to have to go out and buy new calipers (@ $20/each) just to find out that there is isn't them and something else.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Brian84SE||08-22-2000||10:33 PM||||I think the fact that you got brake fluid on the right front has alot to do with the left front "pulling".   It may not be that the LF is braking too much, but that the RF is not braking enough due to fluid contamination on the pads.  Try taking the pads off and spraying them with a brake cleaner, and lightly sand them. If they still smell or feel like they have fluid on them, throw them out and replace them! ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||08-22-2000||10:37 PM||||I'll try that.  I'd hate to spend the extra $$, but if that's what it takes...Does this mean that I'll need new pads, too??||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||08-23-2000||01:18 PM||||Well, I'm off to Autozone to buy the following:  new rotor, new pads and (if I can get them) new rubber lines for the front.  I'll reinstall same and rebleed everything.  If that doesn't work (I have until 10pm to get this done as I've transferred to another store-same chain-and it's about 2 miles from the house), I'll trade the car for a bicycle.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Monkeyman||08-23-2000||02:01 PM||||Did some checking and nobody (not even the dealers) have the brake lines, so I went ahead and ordered the SS ones from the Fiero Store.  At a total of $104.46, my wife will kill me, but at least I won't have to worry about one of them blowing and getting myself killed that way.||||reg||1
Z||000013||JohnnyK||08-23-2000||03:11 PM||||$20 for calipers?? You lucky bastard!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> They are $40-some for the front and about 90some for the rear here (that is Canadian, but still) ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Standard||08-23-2000||04:12 PM||||Total of $104??  For the Earl's SS brake lines?  If I remember right my total came to about $95 when I ordered them, which was cheaper than a set of replacement rubber hoses.  I know mine cost under $100...<P>------------------<BR>Mike -- Golden Valley, MN<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 149,000 miles and counting!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Monkeyman||08-23-2000||05:28 PM||||JohnnyK--<B>'88 Grand Am</B> calipers.  You use the front calipers on both ends of the Fiero.  Fairly common upgrade.  The rotor diameter is the same, but the GA rotors are vented and the GA calipers piston is 5mm larger.  OEM calipers <I>are</I> pretty expensive.  This is one of the reasons why I went with the upgrade.  I needed completely new brakes and this didn't cost that much more.<P>Standard--That includes next day delivery.  I'm gonna try to get them installed tomorrow (then bleed the damn things all over again <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">).<P>Update--Pretty much finished with the brakes.  I installed new pads on the front (wanted to make sure everything was even) and a new rotor on the "oops" side.  Bleed the heck out of them (about a 1/2 gallon total) and took it for a test drive.  I'm still not satisfied with the pedal feel although it's better than before.  The good part is that it doesn't pull.  I'm hoping that the new brake lines will help with the pedal feel and travel.<P>Anyone else here done the upgrade???  How does you pedal feel?  About 1/2 way to the floor I get so-so brakes and if I stand on it, I stop on a quarter (not quite as good as a dime but much better than the OEM brakes which would qualify as a $100 bill).  I guess I was hoping to get brakes like what's on my wifes '98 Cav.  You barely touch those and you're stopped.  I guess not on a Fiero no matter what brakes I use.||||reg||1
Z||000016||BBBAD GT||08-23-2000||06:02 PM||||MM<P>To make you feel better, I installed exactly what you have 6 months ago (88 GA on all four w/Blazer MC).  I wasn't completely "wow'd" by them, but it was better than I had before.  I have <B>exactly</B> the same situation, its a little mushey on the first 25% of travel.  Its nothing compared to the 11" brakes on my V8 Fiero (that thing is incredible), but keep going with the pedel and you can stop predy darn fast.  I was planning on doing the SS brake lines also, but haven't yet (guess I've gotten use to it), so tell me how it feels afterwards.<P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/BBBAD_GT_SIG.gif">   <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><BR><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Monkeyman||08-24-2000||06:49 AM||||Will do.  I'm hoping some of the mushiness is caused by old, worn out mushy lines.  We'll see.  Seems like there was someone on here that had done the conversion who said they could stop on a penny.  Don't remember who it was, though??||||reg||1
Z||000018||ThatRickGuy||08-24-2000||09:56 AM||||I'd say, be happy! My '88 needs a brake job, it too has that week/mushy feel to it (almost identical to what your saying, you really have to stand on it to get it to stop fast) Your parts are WAY CHEEPER then replacing the '88 parts. I think I'm going to go with the bored over MC and the larger calipers w/ the cross drilled rotors, all in all, way too much money, but from what I hear it's some great breaks.||||reg||1
Z||000019||bHooper||08-24-2000||10:03 AM||||MM, after you get back from your trip, b4 you put the new brake lines on, you should purchase a product called <B>Super Blue</B> it is a brake fluid used in race/high dollar cars.<P>Remove all of the DOT 3 (or whatever) you are using, from the system.  Blow out the lines (compressed air), you may even want to flush them with alcohol (popov is fine (jk)), b4 putting this stuff in.  this stuff has a high tollerance for heat, also.<P>i have heard wonderfull things about this stuff, it is about $20 a half gallon... and i plan on using it after i do whatever it is i am going to do on my brakes.<P>hope this helps.<BR><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000020||Monkeyman||08-24-2000||01:29 PM||||How can I flush the brake lines if I don't have any access to compressed air?  Can I just run alcohol through them to squeeze the old (actually new now) brake fluid out?  I'm using some DOT3/DOT4 stuff.  Seems like it's synthetic, but probably not.  Too tired to go out in the garage and look at the remaining bottles.  (I got 4 extra 32 oz bottles just to be sure I had enough.)  Whatever stuff I'm currently using must be fairly decent stuff as it cost about the same as the Super Blue.<P>What do I need to check to be sure that my hard brake lines are still in good shape??  After all of this time, effort, frustration and cash, I want to make sure that my braking system is 100.3% as perfect as I can get it.  So far <B>everything</B> (with the exception of the hard lines) is (or will be) brand spanking new.  <P>What's the best way to get the dust shield out of the way?  The left front rotor is rubbing against it.  I tried grabbing onto it with a pair of Channel Locks, but all I succeeded in doing was bending the lip.||||reg||1
